            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Before the new one: say the German for the baking dish, then the German for to brown."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "input",
          "title": "You'll want to know: köcheln",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "köcheln — \"to simmer\"."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Die Suppe köchelte eine Stunde. The soup simmered for an hour."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "A small form of kochen: gentle cooking."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "etymology",
          "title": "The word, taken apart",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "köcheln is built on kochen \"to cook\", Old High German kochōn, which EtymDB traces to Latin coquus \"a cook\", from coquere \"to cook\"; the ending -eln makes it small and gentle."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
